**(i) Process (1 mark).** **Booting** (booting up, or the bootstrap process; a start-up from power-off is a cold boot).

**(ii) Where the instructions are stored (1 mark).** In the **BIOS**, stored in a ROM (flash ROM) chip on the motherboard, which is non-volatile and so keeps the start-up instructions when the power is off.

**(iii) Two possible reasons for the wait (4 marks, 2 each).**

1. The computer carries out the power-on self-test and the identification of all the connected devices (RAM, keyboard, disks, drives, network card, USB devices); the more hardware there is, the longer this takes.
2. The operating system is large and has to be loaded from the slow hard disk into the RAM; the start-up programs, drivers, services and antivirus that start automatically also have to be loaded, and the more there are, the longer the wait. (Other acceptable reasons: little RAM, a fragmented or slow disk, or a virus infection.)

**(iv) Operating system (2 marks).** An operating system is the system software that manages the hardware and software resources of the computer and acts as the interface between the user (and the application programs) and the hardware, so that the computer can be used (for example Windows, Linux, Android).

**(v) Three functions (3 marks, 1 each).** Any three of:

- memory management (allocating and freeing RAM for programs);
- processor management / scheduling (sharing CPU time among tasks, multitasking);
- file management (organising, naming, storing, retrieving files);
- device management (controlling input/output devices through drivers);
- providing the user interface (command line or graphical);
- security (user accounts, passwords, access rights);
- error handling and reporting.
